Branch-local material for REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW ... WHERE ...
================================================================

This directory is NOT part of any test schedule and is not wired into either
build system.  Delete it before posting the patch to -hackers.  It holds the
things that do not belong in the patch itself:

  PLAN.md       Scoped work in three phases: what the tests need before the
                implementation can change safely, the change itself (string-built
                SQL through SPI -> Query trees), and the performance work that
                only makes sense afterwards.  Read this second.

  ISSUES.md     Review items, split into those raised on the -hackers thread
                (A1-A9) and those found separately (B1-B17), each mapped to the
                test that covers it.  Start here.

  SAFETY.md     Which predicates are safe, established by differential testing
                rather than by argument.  Five independent failure modes, what
                the planner's own push-down analysis already decides, what is
                provable and what is not, and what a static check would cost.

  USE-CASES.md  53 use cases across 17 domains, each placed against a measured
                cost model, with the driver pattern and refresh form that suits
                it.  Intended to feed the documentation and the design
                discussion.

  safety/       The differential safety oracle.  See below.

  run.sh        The lock-ordering stress reproducer.  See below.

All timings in these files come from a -O2 build with assertions off unless they
say otherwise.  An earlier revision quoted debug-build numbers under the caveat
"treat ratios as transferable"; that caveat did not hold, and several ratios
moved materially when re-measured.  Do not add a timing here without recording
which build produced it.


safety/ -- the differential oracle
----------------------------------

    safety/run.sh [PGPORT] [PGDATABASE]        # PSQL_BIN=... to choose a psql

For each (view shape, predicate, mutation): build the matview, mutate the base
data, partial refresh, snapshot, full refresh, symmetric difference.  A
non-empty difference is a counterexample -- proof that the predicate is unsafe
for that view.  An empty one is evidence, not proof.

Two harnesses share the oracle.  The single-shot one covers 42 (view, predicate,
mutation) triples and also records what the planner did with the predicate.  The
exhaustive one takes 18 (view, predicate) pairs and runs each against its
complete single-row mutation space over a 12-row base table -- about 2800
refresh cycles.  Both refresh forms are run for every case, and they have agreed
on every shape so far.

It found the use-after-free that is now B14 before it answered any question
about predicates.


run.sh -- the lock-ordering reproducer
--------------------------------------

    ./run.sh [PGPORT] [PGHOST] [PGDATABASE]

Defaults: port 5432, host /tmp, database postgres.  Requires psql on PATH.

This reproduces A5's non-deterministic variant, which cannot be expressed as an
isolation spec.  The row-locking step of refresh_by_direct_modification() locks
the rows its predicate selects; with no ORDER BY it locks them in whatever order
the plan produces, so two concurrent refreshes with different plans over
overlapping rows deadlock.  Driving that needs both statements simultaneously
mid-scan, which isolationtester cannot arrange -- it steps one session at a
time.

The script builds a matview whose physical row order is the reverse of its key
order, then runs two sessions in parallel: one whose predicate the planner
satisfies with an index scan (ascending key order), and one whose predicate
forces a sequential scan (descending key order).  It exits non-zero and prints a
count if any deadlock is detected.

A5 is now FIXED -- the locking SELECT and the CTE both ORDER BY the arbiter key
-- so this script should exit zero on the current tree.  It is kept as a
regression guard for the life of this branch.  On the tree as originally posted,
roughly half the refreshes aborted.

The ORDER BY fixes only this variant.  For the cross-statement variant, in
src/test/isolation/specs/matview-where-deadlock.spec, it does not help:
ordering within one statement says nothing about the order of separate
statements.
